C LB
"Load buffering r1=x; y=1; || r2=y; x=1;  outcome r1=r2=1. RAO ('Relaxed Atomic +
 Ordering', Saraswat et al. 2007) explains relaxed behaviour by applying
 reorderings/transformations OVER a sequentially consistent execution, so it
 admits each independent read/write being reordered and ALLOWS this LB outcome.
 C11 with all-relaxed atomics also allows plain LB, so this exact program does
 NOT separate them: the separation appears only where C11 uses stronger
 (non-atomic / SC) modes that forbid the reorder while RAO -- which has no mode
 system, only uniform transformations -- still admits it. RAO also CLAIMS the
 external DRF guarantee while permitting thin-air, two properties shown
 incompatible in general (Batty et al., ESOP 2015); the survey attributes the
 claim to a restricted input language (no general conditionals). Both verdicts
 are model-level / CITED -- RAO has no herd7 model and no published litmus
 isolates a single C11-separating outcome. See README."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(y,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (0:r1=1 /\ 1:r2=1)
